  <content>
  <section><title>Activity 
</title>
<para>Make sense of Diego’s work and discuss with a partner, then answer questions 1 - 3.</para>

<para>\(\left\{\begin{array}{l}2x\;-\;3y\;=\;-4\\2x\;+\;3y\;=\;8\end{array}\right.\)</para>

<para>
\(4x = 4\\
  x = 1\\
  2(1) + 3y = 8\\
  3y = 6\\
  y = 2
  \)
</para>

<!--Q#1-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> What did Diego do to solve the system? </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. Diego added the two equations, and the sum is an equation with only one variable, \(x\), which can be solved.  He then substituted the \(x\)-value into the second equation and solved for \(y\).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="332656da-9c94-4a72-80e4-fc18b7d3367c" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> Is the pair of \(x\)- and \(y\)-values that Diego found actually a solution to the system?  </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The answer is yes.</para><para>Try again. The answer is yes.</para></solution></exercise></section>
<!--Q#3-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> How do you know? </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para> When the values are substituted back to the equations in the system, they make the equations true.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<para>For questions 4 – 7 determine if Diego’s method will work for solving the system. Be prepared to show your reasoning. If Diego’s method does not work, use a different method to solve and get the answers.</para>
<para>\(\left\{\begin{array}{l}\begin{array}{rcl}2x+y&amp;=&amp;4\\x-y&amp;=&amp;11\end{array}\end{array}\right.\)</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="a1c65390-c43c-4b57-8f14-96cd29348568" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> Will Diego’s method work for solving the system? </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The answer is yes.</para><para>The answer is yes.</para></solution></exercise></section><section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="fe7c7f26-3863-4651-9e81-6e44d1190d77" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here.</para><para> What is the \(x\) value? </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The answer is 5.</para><para>The answer is 5.</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="b4a6ed6d-091f-482c-ba30-81085479bb05" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here.</para><para> What is the \(y\) value? </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The answer is -6.</para><para>The answer is -6.</para></solution></exercise></section>
<!--Q#7-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Explain your solutions. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. Adding the two equations gives \(3x=15\), or \(x=5\). Substituting 5 for \(x\) in either one of the original equations gives \(y=−6\).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<para>For questions 8 – 11 determine if Diego’s method will work for solving the system. Be prepared to show your reasoning. If Diego’s method does not work, use a different method to solve and get the answers.</para>
<para>\(\left\{\begin{array}{lc}\begin{array}{rcl}8x+11y&amp;=&amp;37\;\;\\8x+y&amp;=&amp;7\end{array}\end{array}\right.\)</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="77cd723b-2ccc-4320-aa59-ec709732e366" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> Will Diego’s method work for solving the system? </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The answer is no.</para><para>The answer is no.</para></solution></exercise></section>
<!--Q#9-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> What is the \(x\) value? </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <newline/><para> \(\frac12\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="20909679-742b-4d6b-a5eb-04c5fdd04a82" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here.</para><para> What is the \(y\) value? </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The answer is 3. </para><para>The answer is 3.</para></solution></exercise></section>
<!--Q#11-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Explain your solutions. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. Diego’s method does not work because adding the two equations doesn’t get us anywhere. The sum is \(16x+12y=44\), which doesn’t make it easier to solve.</para>
    
    <para>It works if the second equation is subtracted from the first instead of added to the first. The difference is \(10y=30\), or \(y=3\). If we substitute 3 for \(y\) in one of the original equations, we get \(x=\frac12\).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
